  "variants": [
    {
      "persona": "Whitehat Hacker",
      "author": "Asterlink Incident Desk <incident@asterlinktransit.com>",
      "subject": "Final ECHO-73 scan report",
      "shortSummary": "Final report for the Asterlink ECHO-73 finding.",
      "date": "0001-01-01T00:00:00",
      "body": "{player},\n\nFinal finding: ECHO-73 is a vendor diagnostics appliance at 10.42.18.73 in the approved IPv4 range. It exposes RailGate Diagnostics 3.4.7 on TCP/8080, was also observed responding at IPv6 address fd42:18::73, and initiated a TLS session to ticketing host 10.42.22.41. Recommended action: isolate ECHO-73, remove unnecessary diagnostics exposure, restrict vendor access, and add internal monitoring at the ticketing boundary. Confirm authorization separately before any further IPv6 testing.\n\nMira has accepted the report into the incident channel.",
      "attachements": []
    },
    {
      "persona": "Blackhat Hacker",
      "author": "Asterlink Incident Desk <incident@asterlinktransit.com>",
      "subject": "Final ECHO-73 scan report",
      "shortSummary": "Final report for the Asterlink ECHO-73 finding.",
      "date": "0001-01-01T00:00:00",
      "body": "{player},\n\nFinal map retained: ECHO-73 is a vendor diagnostics appliance at 10.42.18.73, with an observed IPv6 address of fd42:18::73, RailGate Diagnostics 3.4.7 on TCP/8080, and a TLS session to ticketing host 10.42.22.41. The report can guide remediation, but the same verified detail would be dangerous in the wrong hands.\n\nKade knows exactly why it has value.",
      "attachements": []
    },
    {
      "persona": "Hacktivist",
      "author": "Asterlink Incident Desk <incident@asterlinktransit.com>",
      "subject": "Final ECHO-73 scan report",
      "shortSummary": "Final report for the Asterlink ECHO-73 finding.",
      "date": "0001-01-01T00:00:00",
      "body": "{player},\n\nFinal redacted summary: a vendor diagnostics appliance in the launch network initiated a session to ticketing, and the internal ticketing boundary lacked sensor coverage. Live addresses, service strings, and packet-level route details remain withheld from the public version while Asterlink receives a short fix window.\n\nLena is ready to push for accountability without publishing an attack map.",
      "attachements": []
    }
  ]
}
